    ns208:test_bound_bnode_class_check_is_correct_on_native_backend dcterms:identifier "tests.shacl.integration.test_native_backend_oxigraph.test_bound_bnode_class_check_is_correct_on_native_backend" ;
        a slkg:Function ;
        rdfs:comment """A real, well-formed sh:rule blank node (rdf:type sh:TripleRule) used
to be misreported as failing meta-shacl's own rdf:type check when its
shapes graph was native-backed - traced to StarLayerGraph._native_triples()
(in starlayer.graph, not this file) unconditionally skolemizing a bound
BNode before querying, which only matches content written via the
single-triple add() path - parse() (used here, and by every realistic
shapes-graph load) goes through _native_add_many() instead, which
deliberately keeps real, unskolemized blank nodes. Fixed in
starlayer.graph itself; this locks in the SHACL-level symptom that
surfaced it: a plain rdf:type lookup on a parsed blank node must return
what's actually there, not nothing.""" ;
        rdfs:label "test_bound_bnode_class_check_is_correct_on_native_backend" ;
        slkg:calls ns208:_native_graph ;
        slkg:filePath "packages/starlayer/tests/shacl/integration/test_native_backend_oxigraph.py" ;
        slkg:hasReturnValue ns208:test_bound_bnode_class_check_is_correct_on_native_backend.returns ;
        slkg:lineEnd 255 ;
        slkg:lineStart 232 ;
        slkg:signature "def test_bound_bnode_class_check_is_correct_on_native_backend() -> None" .

    ns208:test_bound_bnode_class_check_is_correct_on_native_backend.returns a slkg:ReturnValue ;
        slkg:annotationText "None" ;
        slkg:optional true .

    ns208:test_global_sparql_rule_produces_triples_on_native_backend dcterms:identifier "tests.shacl.integration.test_native_backend_oxigraph.test_global_sparql_rule_produces_triples_on_native_backend" ;
        a slkg:Function ;
        rdfs:comment """Found via direct testing against a real Oxigraph/Fuseki instance:
``_global_sparql_rule_triples``'s ``_apply_one`` ran its CONSTRUCT query
via ``data_graph.query(...)`` with no graph-scoping of its own -
``starlayer.graph.backends.native.native_query()`` sends a query's text
to the remote endpoint completely unscoped (see that function's own
docstring), so an ordinary, no-``GRAPH``-clause CONSTRUCT like this
one silently matched against the store's separate, empty default graph
instead of the data graph's own named context, and produced *nothing*
- confirmed directly against both Oxigraph and Fuseki, and confirmed
that adding an explicit ``GRAPH <identifier>`` wrapper around the same
query fixes it. Fixed by snapshotting a native ``data_graph``'s current
triples into a local, in-memory ``StarLayerGraph`` and evaluating the
CONSTRUCT there instead (correct regardless of query complexity, unlike
a textual ``GRAPH`` wrapper, which would need real SPARQL-aware
rewriting for anything beyond a simple BGP). Shape-attached
``sh:TripleRule``/``sh:SPARQLRule`` execution was already confirmed
working correctly on a native backend - this gap was specific to the
global (shape-independent) rules path.""" ;
        rdfs:label "test_global_sparql_rule_produces_triples_on_native_backend" ;
        slkg:calls ns208:_native_graph ;
        slkg:filePath "packages/starlayer/tests/shacl/integration/test_native_backend_oxigraph.py" ;
        slkg:hasReturnValue ns208:test_global_sparql_rule_produces_triples_on_native_backend.returns ;
        slkg:instantiates ns28:StarLayerGraph ;
        slkg:lineEnd 229 ;
        slkg:lineStart 186 ;
        slkg:signature "def test_global_sparql_rule_produces_triples_on_native_backend() -> None" .

    ns208:test_global_sparql_rule_produces_triples_on_native_backend.returns a slkg:ReturnValue ;
        slkg:annotationText "None" ;
        slkg:optional true .

    ns208:test_meta_shacl_reports_a_genuine_blank_node_violation_without_crashing dcterms:identifier "tests.shacl.integration.test_native_backend_oxigraph.test_meta_shacl_reports_a_genuine_blank_node_violation_without_crashing" ;
        a slkg:Function ;
        rdfs:comment """The other half of the report-stringification fix: a *real* violation
whose focus node is a blank node must still be reportable (not just
silently avoided) once meta-shacl no longer misfires on well-formed
shapes. A rule missing sh:predicate/sh:object is genuinely malformed -
the resulting error message must actually name the blank node's real
content, not crash trying to.""" ;
        rdfs:label "test_meta_shacl_reports_a_genuine_blank_node_violation_without_crashing" ;
        slkg:calls ns208:_native_graph ;
