    ns76:_cross_node_reachable_shapes dcterms:identifier "starlayer.shacl.native_components._cross_node_reachable_shapes" ;
        a slkg:Function ;
        rdfs:comment """Every shape node in ``shapes_graph`` whose composition tree - walked
transitively through ``_COMPOSITION_PREDICATES`` - reaches a shape that
directly declares one of ``_CROSS_NODE_PREDICATES``.

Computed once per distinct shapes-graph object and cached in a
``WeakKeyDictionary`` (note: rdflib's ``Graph.__eq__``/``__hash__`` key
off ``identifier``, not Python object identity - harmless in practice
since ``encode_graph()`` never sets an explicit ``identifier``, so each
call gets rdflib's own fresh-random-BNode default, but this is a
same-*content* cache, not a same-*object* cache, so don't rely on the
latter). The entry is dropped once the graph is garbage-collected - no
explicit invalidation needed. This is what lets nested composition (e.g.
``sh:and`` containing an ``sh:and`` containing the shape that declares
``sh:uniqueValuesFor``) be detected correctly: a shallow, one-level
check on the immediate child shape misses this, since the child itself
doesn't declare the cross-node predicate - only something *it* composes
does. Every registered component evaluating shapes from the same
``validate()`` call shares the identical (encoded) shapes graph object,
so this table is computed once per ``validate()`` call and then just
looked up, not re-walked live on every branch decision.

Computed via monotonic fixed-point propagation, not DFS-with-memo: a
naive DFS that memoizes a node's answer using a cycle-guard's early
``False`` return is unsound whenever the composition graph has a real
cycle. Counterexample: ``A`` composes ``B``, ``B`` composes ``A``, and
``A`` also composes a shape ``C`` that declares the cross-node
predicate. ``B``'s correct answer is True (via A -> C), but a DFS
starting at A that visits B before C would hit the cycle guard while
exploring B (B -> A, A already on the stack) and permanently memoize B
as False before C is ever considered - silently reintroducing the exact
vacuous-conformance bug this whole mechanism exists to prevent. This
isn't a merely theoretical gap either: Python set iteration order isn't
guaranteed, so whether a given run visits B before or after C - and
therefore whether it gets this wrong - can vary across runs. A
worklist that repeatedly marks a node reachable if anything it composes
is already marked, until nothing changes, is correct regardless of
iteration order or how the cycle is shaped.""" ;
        rdfs:label "_cross_node_reachable_shapes" ;
        slkg:calls ns76:_shapes_directly_composed_by ;
        slkg:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/shacl/native_components.py" ;
        slkg:hasParameter ns76:_cross_node_reachable_shapes.param.shapes_graph ;
        slkg:hasReturnValue ns76:_cross_node_reachable_shapes.returns ;
        slkg:isPrivate true ;
        slkg:lineEnd 967 ;
        slkg:lineStart 899 ;
        slkg:signature "def _cross_node_reachable_shapes(shapes_graph: Any) -> frozenset" .

    ns76:_cross_node_reachable_shapes.param.shapes_graph a slkg:Parameter ;
        rdfs:label "shapes_graph" ;
        slkg:annotationText "Any" ;
        slkg:hasType ns4:typing.Any ;
        slkg:parameterKind "positional" ;
        slkg:position 0 .

    ns76:_cross_node_reachable_shapes.returns a slkg:ReturnValue ;
        slkg:annotationText "frozenset" ;
        slkg:hasType ns4:builtins.frozenset .

