GRAPH <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/blob/main/packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/graph/_timeout.py> {
    ns25:_timeout.py dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.graph._timeout" ;
        a slkg:Module ;
        rdfs:comment """starlayer.graph.graph._timeout

A general wall-clock timeout for OWL-DL reasoning calls
(`infer(entailment=ENTAILMENT["OWL-Direct"], engine=...)`, either engine) - defense-in-depth
against a hang no pre-flight structural check has been written for yet.
`owl_dl_rustdl.py`'s own `_check_rustdl_hang_risk()` covers one known,
specific hang pattern (fast, a few milliseconds, a clear "here's exactly
what's wrong" message); this covers everything else, for either engine,
known pattern or not - the two are complementary, not alternatives to
each other.

**Why a subprocess, not a thread + `signal.alarm()`/`threading.Event`:**
the actual blocking call in each engine is either a JVM subprocess wait
(HermiT, via `owlready2.sync_reasoner()`) or an opaque, uninterruptible
native PyO3 call with no Python bytecode execution in between it and
whatever it's doing internally (RustDL). Neither reliably yields back to
Python's own interpreter loop for a signal handler to fire, and a
background *thread* running either one can't actually be killed from
Python once started - only the *caller* gives up waiting on it; the
thread, and whatever it's blocked on, keep running (and consuming
CPU/memory/a JVM child process) indefinitely in the background regardless.
The only reliable way to bound an opaque, uninterruptible blocking call is
OS-level process termination.

**Why a full process *group* kill, not just the immediate child:**
HermiT's own call chain is Python -> owlready2 (the subprocess this module
spawns) -> a further `java` subprocess owlready2 launches itself. Killing
only the immediate child leaves that `java` grandchild running, orphaned,
still consuming resources indefinitely - exactly the orphaned-process bug
class already found and fixed elsewhere in this project. `run_with_timeout()`
makes the child call `os.setpgrp()` as its very first action (before doing
any real work), making it - and therefore anything it further spawns -
the leader of a brand-new process group distinct from the parent's;
`os.killpg()` on timeout then reliably takes the whole tree down with one
signal, not just the direct child.

**POSIX only** (`os.setpgrp`/`os.killpg` have no Windows equivalent) - the
same implicit scope every other subprocess-based piece of this codebase
already has (e.g. `shutil.which("java")`-style checks are portable, but
process-group semantics are not); not something this project has taken on
Windows support for elsewhere either.

**`multiprocessing`'s "spawn" start method, explicitly, not the platform
default:** deterministic across macOS/Linux rather than relying on
whichever start method happens to be each platform's own default (macOS
has used "spawn" as its own default since Python 3.8 specifically because
"fork" is documented as unsafe once certain system libraries - notably
anything Objective-C-runtime-adjacent - have already been loaded; forking
a process that may have JVM/native library state loaded is a related,
real risk this module has no reason to accept just because some other
platform's default might still be "fork"). The cost is that `target` must
be picklable by reference (a real module-level function, not a
closure/lambda) - already true of every caller in this codebase.""" ;
        rdfs:label "_timeout" ;
        slkg:declares ns26:_kill_process_group, ns26:run_with_timeout, ns26:_trampoline, ns26:ReasoningTimeoutError ;
        slkg:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/graph/_timeout.py" ;
        slkg:importsModule ns4:os, ns4:typing, ns4:signal, ns4:collections.abc, ns4:__future__, ns4:multiprocessing, ns4:time .

    ns26:ReasoningTimeoutError dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.graph._timeout.ReasoningTimeoutError" ;
        a slkg:Class ;
        rdfs:comment """Raised when an OWL-DL reasoning call (either engine) exceeds its
wall-clock budget and had to be forcibly killed - see this module's
own docstring for why a hard process-group kill, not a cooperative
cancellation, is the only mechanism available here.""" ;
        rdfs:label "ReasoningTimeoutError" ;
        rdfs:subClassOf ns4:builtins.RuntimeError ;
        slkg:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/graph/_timeout.py" ;
        slkg:lineEnd 75 ;
        slkg:lineStart 71 .

    ns26:_kill_process_group dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.graph._timeout._kill_process_group" ;
        a slkg:Function ;
        rdfs:comment """SIGKILL the entire process group rooted at `pid` (which is a valid
process-group ID only because `_trampoline` above called
`os.setpgrp()` as the child's first action) - not just `pid` itself -
so a JVM (or other) grandchild the reasoning call spawned is never
left orphaned and running. Safe to call on an already-dead process.""" ;
        rdfs:label "_kill_process_group" ;
        slkg:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/graph/_timeout.py" ;
        slkg:hasParameter ns26:_kill_process_group.param.pid ;
        slkg:hasReturnValue ns26:_kill_process_group.returns ;
        slkg:isPrivate true ;
        slkg:lineEnd 193 ;
        slkg:lineStart 179 ;
        slkg:signature "def _kill_process_group(pid: int) -> None" .

    ns26:_kill_process_group.param.pid a slkg:Parameter ;
        rdfs:comment "Process ID of the child process whose process group should be killed." ;
        rdfs:label "pid" ;
        slkg:annotationText "int" ;
        slkg:hasType ns4:builtins.int ;
        slkg:parameterKind "positional" ;
        slkg:position 0 .

    ns26:_kill_process_group.returns a slkg:ReturnValue ;
        slkg:annotationText "None" ;
        slkg:optional true .

    ns26:_trampoline dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.graph._timeout._trampoline" ;
        a slkg:Function ;
        rdfs:comment """Runs inside the child process, before `target` itself. `os.setpgrp()`
here - not left to `target` to remember - is what makes the
process-group kill below actually work; centralizing it here means no
future `target` implementation can forget it and silently reintroduce
the orphaned-JVM-process bug this module exists to prevent.""" ;
        rdfs:label "_trampoline" ;
        slkg:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/graph/_timeout.py" ;
        slkg:hasParameter ns26:_trampoline.param.args, ns26:_trampoline.param.target, ns26:_trampoline.param.queue ;
        slkg:hasReturnValue ns26:_trampoline.returns ;
        slkg:isPrivate true ;
        slkg:lineEnd 93 ;
        slkg:lineStart 78 ;
        slkg:signature "def _trampoline(target: Callable[..., None], args: tuple, queue) -> None" .

    ns26:_trampoline.param.args a slkg:Parameter ;
        rdfs:comment "Positional arguments to pass to `target`." ;
        rdfs:label "args" ;
        slkg:annotationText "tuple" ;
        slkg:hasType ns4:builtins.tuple ;
        slkg:parameterKind "positional" ;
        slkg:position 1 .

    ns26:_trampoline.param.queue a slkg:Parameter ;
        rdfs:comment "The multiprocessing queue `target` puts its result tuple onto." ;
        rdfs:label "queue" ;
        slkg:parameterKind "positional" ;
        slkg:position 2 .

    ns26:_trampoline.param.target a slkg:Parameter ;
        rdfs:comment "The module-level function to run, called as `target(*args, queue)`." ;
        rdfs:label "target" ;
        slkg:annotationText "Callable[..., None]" ;
        slkg:hasType ns4:collections.abc.Callable ;
        slkg:parameterKind "positional" ;
        slkg:position 0 .

    ns26:_trampoline.returns a slkg:ReturnValue ;
        slkg:annotationText "None" ;
        slkg:optional true .

    ns26:run_with_timeout dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.graph._timeout.run_with_timeout" ;
        a slkg:Function ;
        rdfs:comment """Run `target(*args, queue)` in a fresh child process (its own
process group) with a hard wall-clock budget.

`target` must be a module-level (picklable-by-reference) function that
does its own work and puts exactly one result tuple onto `queue` when
done - this function itself never inspects the *content* of that
tuple (each engine's own module defines its own small result-status
protocol), only whether one arrived before the deadline.""" ;
        rdfs:label "run_with_timeout" ;
        slkg:calls ns26:_kill_process_group ;
        slkg:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/graph/_timeout.py" ;
        slkg:hasParameter ns26:run_with_timeout.param.timeout_seconds, ns26:run_with_timeout.param.args, ns26:run_with_timeout.param.target ;
        slkg:hasReturnValue ns26:run_with_timeout.returns ;
        slkg:instantiates ns26:ReasoningTimeoutError ;
        slkg:lineEnd 176 ;
        slkg:lineStart 96 ;
        slkg:signature "def run_with_timeout(target: Callable[..., None], args: tuple, timeout_seconds: float | None) -> tuple" .

    ns26:run_with_timeout.param.args a slkg:Parameter ;
        rdfs:comment "Positional arguments to pass to `target`, before the implicit trailing `queue`." ;
        rdfs:label "args" ;
        slkg:annotationText "tuple" ;
        slkg:hasType ns4:builtins.tuple ;
        slkg:parameterKind "positional" ;
        slkg:position 1 .

    ns26:run_with_timeout.param.target a slkg:Parameter ;
        rdfs:comment "A module-level (picklable-by-reference) function, called as `target(*args, queue)` in the child process." ;
        rdfs:label "target" ;
        slkg:annotationText "Callable[..., None]" ;
        slkg:hasType ns4:collections.abc.Callable ;
        slkg:parameterKind "positional" ;
        slkg:position 0 .

    ns26:run_with_timeout.param.timeout_seconds a slkg:Parameter ;
        rdfs:comment "Wall-clock budget in seconds. `None` disables the timeout entirely (waits indefinitely, the pre-this-feature behavior) - an explicit opt-out, not the default; every real caller in this codebase passes `DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` unless the caller of *that* overrides it." ;
        rdfs:label "timeout_seconds" ;
        slkg:annotationText "float | None" ;
        slkg:hasType ns4:builtins.float ;
        slkg:optional true ;
        slkg:parameterKind "positional" ;
        slkg:position 2 .

    ns26:run_with_timeout.returns a slkg:ReturnValue ;
        rdfs:comment "Whatever `target` put on the queue. Raises ReasoningTimeoutError (after killing the child's entire process group) if nothing arrives within `timeout_seconds`." ;
        slkg:annotationText "tuple" ;
        slkg:hasType ns4:builtins.tuple .

    ns4:__future__ a slkg:ExternalModule .

